- Retrieved practices:
- Avoid invalid pointer-to-member access in C++
WHAT: Using .* or ->* with a pointer-to-member that refers to a member not present in the dynamic type of the object, or using a null pointer-to-member, causes undefined behavior.
WHY: Undefined behavior can lead to memory corruption, crashes, or exploitable security vulnerabilities.
HOW: Ensure the dynamic type of the object actually defines the targeted member before using .* or ->*; never use null pointer-to-member values; avoid casting pointer-to-member values across unrelated types.
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe): `void (Base::*pm)() = nullptr; (obj.*pm)();` After (safe): `void (Base::*pm)() = &Base::f; if (pm) (obj.*pm)();`

- Do not create std::string from a null pointer
WHAT: Constructing std::string directly from functions that may return null (for example, std::getenv()) causes undefined behavior when the returned pointer is null.
WHY: Undefined behavior can lead to crashes, data corruption, or exploitable conditions in security-sensitive code.
HOW: Always check the returned pointer for null before constructing std::string; if it is null, use a safe default (such as an empty string) or handle the error path explicitly.
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe): `std::string tmp(std::getenv("TMP"));` After (safe): `const char* p = std::getenv("TMP"); std::string tmp(p ? p : "");`

- Avoid information leakage when passing C++ class objects across trust boundaries
WHAT: Passing raw C++ class objects (by value or raw memory copy) across a trust boundary can leak sensitive data via padding bits and unused bit-fields whose contents are implementation-defined.
WHY: Attackers receiving such objects may read uninitialized padding/bit-field data, exposing prior in-memory values (keys, pointers, secrets) and enabling information disclosure or further exploitation.
HOW: Do not transmit raw class layouts; instead, define explicit serialized representations (e.g., structs without padding, versioned wire formats) and copy only initialized fields into these representations before sending across trust boundaries.
EXAMPLE: Before: `send(sock, &obj, sizeof(obj), 0);` After: define a packed/serialized DTO with only needed fields, populate it from `obj`, then `send(sock, &dto, sizeof(dto), 0);`

- Avoid information leakage when passing objects across trust boundaries
WHAT: Failing to fully initialize all fields (including padding and reserved fields) of objects or structs before copying them across a trust boundary can leak uninitialized memory contents.
WHY: Attackers can read sensitive kernel or process memory, potentially exposing secrets, internal state, or enabling further exploitation.
HOW: Before returning or sending any struct/class instance to less-trusted code, explicitly initialize or zero all fields (including padding/reserved areas), or use APIs/types that guarantee zero-initialization.
EXAMPLE (C++): Before: `MyStruct s; some_fields_set(s); send_to_user(s);` After: `MyStruct s{}; some_fields_set(s); secure_memset_unused_fields(s); send_to_user(s);`

- Avoid information leakage in class object padding across trust boundaries
WHAT: Passing a C++ class object (or pointer/reference to it) across a trust boundary can leak sensitive data via uninitialized padding bits inside the object.
WHY: Padding bits may contain indeterminate values from prior memory usage, exposing secrets (keys, passwords, internal state) to less-trusted code or remote parties.
HOW: Before sending or sharing a class object across a trust boundary, ensure all padding is deterministically initialized (for example, zero-initialize the whole storage, then assign fields) or serialize only explicit fields into a separate, fully initialized transfer structure.
EXAMPLE: Before: `MyMsg msg; msg.id = userId; send(&msg, sizeof(msg));` After: `MyMsg msg{}; msg.id = userId; send(&msg, sizeof(msg));` or serialize only `id` into a packed transfer buffer.



